# RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS

Synthesis and characterization of MOFs NAF films. A series of NAF films was constructed using heteroepitaxial growth.28-30 Briefly, \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanostrand films on nylon 66 microporous membranes were first synthesised as previously reported with minor modification (Figure S1).31 These were then converted into NAF films accompanied by oriented growth of organic ligands at low concentrations (Figure 2a and Figures S2-S3). The thickness of the NAF films was regulated

by changing the amount of the \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanostrand precursor. Well-defined NAF films with thicknesses of 1.0, 1.6, 2.9 and \(4.0\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) (according to the length-to-spacing ratio, they are denoted as NAF-0.5, NAF-1, NAF-2 and NAF-3, respectively) were synthesised; these films were assembled of nanosheets with \(\sim25\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick (Figures 2b–2e and Figures S4-S6). The interplanar spacing \((3.0\ \textup{\AA}\) for the (240) plane) of the lattice fringes and diffraction spots for the (240) plane in the electron diffraction pattern confirmed heteroepitaxial growth of MOF nanosheets on \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (Figures 2f–2h).30 Statistical analyses of the test results established that the nanosheet thickness and spacing, and the NAF film surface pore size distribution, were nearly identical for all of the NAF films (Figure S5). For comparison, a bulk-type MOF film with contrasting morphology was manufactured by changing the concentration of the organic ligands (Figure 2i–2j and Figure S7).

The NAF and bulk-type films had identical crystal structures (Figure S8a and S8b) and their ultraviolet–visible diffuse reflectance spectra demonstrated that the effective light absorption thickness of the NAF films was much thinner than that of the bulk-type film (Figure S8c). Furthermore, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy confirmed the similarity of the bulk-type and NAF film compositions (Figures S8d and S9).

