The Pd NCs@MOF NAF film was studied as a proof of concept to verify that the deformable nanosheets increased mass exchange between the film and its environment, thereby facilitating reactant accessibility and accelerating product diffusion. Well-defined and uniform Pd NCs were synthesised as previously reported with (100) facets exposed (Figure 6c and Figure S21).38 The Pd NCs@MOF composites were readily obtained by mixing Pd NCs with the \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) nanostrand film; the well-dispersed Pd NCs were fully encapsulated within the MOF (Figures 6d, S22 and S23). The catalytic performance of the Pd NCs@MOF composites for the hydrogenation of alkenes is shown in Figure 6e. Although all of the Pd NCs@MOF composites had similar accessible active sites (Table S3, Supporting Information), only \(28.5\pm2.8\%\) of hydrogenation product was formed when the Pd NCs were encapsulated in the bulk-type film. The activities of the Pd NCs@NAF-0.5 (conversion of \(67.1\pm1.5\%\) ), Pd NCs@NAF-1 \((84.3\pm2.1\%)\) , Pd NCs@NAF-2 \((97.0\pm2.5\%)\) and Pd NCs@NAF-3 \((92.1\pm3.1\%)\) composites were significantly better for \(n\) -hexene hydrogenation. The higher catalytic efficiency of Pd NCs@MOF NAF compared to the bulk-type Pd NCs@MOF film is attributed to faster mass diffusion via disturbance of the diffusion layer.

The kinetic analyses demonstrated that the NCs@MOF NAF films with relatively high mass diffusion transport rates indeed exhibited much higher catalytic activity than the bulk-type NCs@MOF (Figure S24). Furthermore, the unique assembled structure provided the Pd NCs@MOF NAF with good stability, with only a slight reduction in conversion over three consecutive runs (Figure 6f). The reason for the activity decrease should be attributed to the adsorption of product or reactant in MOF nanosheets, resulting in slight loss in mass transfer efficiency. The NAF nanosheets maintained their assembled frame, and the MOFs covered with Pd NCs remained well dispersed within the nanosheets (Figure S25). The Pd content of the Pd NCs@NAF-2 was determined to be \(7.2\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) after three runs, demonstrating the content of Pd NCs is kept consistent during the catalytic reaction. In contrast, monodispersed Pd NCs became severely aggregated over three recycles and displayed a marked decline in activity (Figure S26). The X-ray diffraction patterns and nitrogen adsorption and desorption isotherms showed that the crystallinity and porosity of the as-synthesised catalyst was preserved in the used catalyst, implying good long-term stability (Figures S27-S29).

# CONCLUSIONS

Comparing the adsorption and catalytic conversion results for the nanosheet-encapsulated Pd NCs and for bulk-type MOF confirmed the power of the deformable catalysis approach in adjusting the mass transport, heterogeneous reactivity and surface structural dynamics of nanocatalysts. Insights into catalytic reaction mechanisms and acceleration of

the interplay between reactants and catalysts will enable the design and development of novel and better catalysts and catalytic processes. For example, varying the Young's modulus to adjust the swing speed of a support or providing self-powered functions could fully convert a key reactant into the desired product.

# EXPERIMENTAL METHODS

Preparation of the bulk-type MOF and NAF films. Equal volumes of a \(4\;\mathrm{mM}\;\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) solution and a \(1.4\;\mathrm{mM}\) aminoethanol solution were mixed rapidly and aged at \(60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . Filtering \(60\,\mathrm{\mL}\) of the mixture solution through a nylon 66 microporous membrane left a light-blue thin film on the membrane. Bulk-type MOF and NAF films were prepared by reacting the obtained film with \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of a \(10\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}\) or 50 mL of a \(0.2\,\mathrm{~g~}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) solution of terephthalic acid in \(N{,}N{,}\) dimethylformamide/water \((10/0.1\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~\mathrm{~v/v~}~}~}\) . This provided additional MOFs with two different morphologies. A pure MOF thin film typically formed after \(^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}\) at room temperature. The membrane holding a pure MOF thin film was vacuum-dried at \(120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight to remove residual solvent.

Preparation of the Pd NCs. The synthesis was carried out by the following route developed by Jiang et al.38 Typically, an aqueous solution \(\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~8~\mL~}~}\) containing \(105~\mathrm{{\mg}}\) of poly(vinyl pyrrolidone) (PVP; MW: 58000), \(60~\mathrm{mg}\) of L-ascorbic acid and \(600~\mathrm{{mg}}\) of KBr was charged into a \(20{\-}{\bmod{L}}\) vial. The solution was preheated under magnetic stirring at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 15 min. Next, \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) of a solution of \(57~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}\) dissolved in water was added by pipette. The reaction proceeded at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) . The products were collected by centrifugation and washed three times with water to remove excess PVP, L-ascorbic acid and KBr.

Encapsulation of Pd NCs in the MOFs. A \(0.4~\mathrm{mL}\) (1 mg \(\mathrm{mL^{-1}}\) ) portion of the prepared Pd NC colloids was mixed with \(60~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) solution and stirred for approximately 5 min. After filtering the mixture solution through a nylon 66 microporous membrane, a thin film of Pd NCs@Cu(OH)2 was obtained. The synthetic route to Pd NCs@MOF composites having different morphologies was similar to that used for the pure MOF described above.

Mechanical property measurement. Mechanical properties were measured by a nanoindenter (TriboScope; Hysitron, USA) using a Berkowich diamond tip, which is a three-sided pyramid with a tip radius of \({\sim}150~\mathrm{nm}\) . The indentation depth was set in the range of \(10\%{-}20\%\) of the thickness of the film to minimise the influence of the substrate on the sample. Seven indentations were made for each sample. The load–displacement curve was obtained at a constant loading/unloading rate of \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{N}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) .

Hydrogenation of alkenes catalysed by the Pd NCs@MOF composites. The catalytic reaction was typically conducted in a two-necked flask containing alkene \((0.5\;\mathrm{mL})\) , \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(7.2~\mathrm{wt}\%\) Pd NCs@MOF or MOF and \(3\;\mathrm{mL}\) of ethyl acetate and purged with a flow of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at 1 atm pressure at room temperature. The flask was purged with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) several times to ensure a pure \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) atmosphere for the reaction and the catalytic hydrogenation was carried out under mechanical stirring. For the catalytic
